      <article class="article-body prose">

        <div class="definition-box" style="margin-top: var(--space-8);">
          <div class="definition-box__label">The Simple Answer</div>
          <p>A marketing budget is annual revenue multiplied by a benchmark percentage. The U.S. Small Business Administration suggests <strong>7–8% of gross revenue</strong> for businesses under $5M with an established product, and the 2026 Gartner CMO Spend Survey found budgets average <strong>7.8% of revenue</strong>. A business with $800,000 in revenue at 8% budgets <strong>$64,000 a year, or $5,333 a month</strong>. Use the <a href="/marketing-budget-calculator/" style="color: var(--teal-dark);">free Marketing Budget Calculator</a> for a figure based on your revenue and industry.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="definition-box" style="margin-top: var(--space-4);">
          <div class="definition-box__label">Key Takeaways</div>
          <ul>
            <li>Percentage method: budget = annual revenue × benchmark % ($800,000 × 8% = $64,000).</li>
            <li>Objective method: work backwards from a revenue goal through close rate and cost per lead.</li>
            <li>CAC method: budget = target new customers × customer acquisition cost.</li>
            <li>Surveys put the average near 7-8% of revenue; growth-stage and startup businesses plan higher.</li>
            <li>Fund two channels properly for 90 days before adding a third.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>

        <h2 id="m-percent">Method 1 — Percentage of Revenue (Most Common)</h2>
        <p>The simplest and most widely used approach: take a percentage of your current or projected revenue and set that as your marketing budget.</p>

        <div class="formula-box">
          <p class="formula-title">Formula</p>
          <p class="formula-text">Marketing Budget = Annual Revenue × Benchmark %</p>
          <div class="formula-example">
            <strong>Example:</strong> $800,000 revenue × 8% = $64,000 annual marketing budget ($5,333/month). At the 12% growth-stage rate the same business budgets $96,000 ($8,000/month).
          </div>
        </div>

        <p><strong>Advantage:</strong> Simple, scales automatically with the business, easy to communicate to stakeholders.</p>
        <p><strong>Limitation:</strong> Doesn't account for growth ambitions. A company trying to double revenue needs to spend more than the maintenance rate. Adjust the percentage upward if you're in growth mode.</p>
        <p>Survey averages give the baseline: Gartner's 2026 CMO Spend Survey of 401 CMOs found 7.8% of revenue (7.7% in 2025), and The CMO Survey's Spring 2026 results (308 respondents) average 9.0% overall and range from 7.0% for B2B product firms to 12.0% for B2C product firms. The full table is on the <a href="/marketing-budget-benchmarks/">Marketing Budget Benchmarks</a> page.</p>

        <h2 id="m-stage">Budget by Business Stage (Planning Ranges)</h2>
        <p>Younger and smaller businesses spend a larger share of revenue on marketing than established ones, because they are building awareness from a small base. The ranges below are planning ranges used on this site, not survey results.</p>
        <div class="bench-table-wrap" style="margin: var(--space-4) 0;">
          <table class="bench-table">
            <thead><tr><th scope="col">Stage</th><th scope="col">Budget % of Revenue</th><th scope="col">Priority Channels</th><th scope="col">Avoid</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
              {stageGuide.map((row, i) => (
                <tr style={i % 2 !== 0 ? 'background: var(--off-white)' : ''}>
                  <td><strong>{row.stage}</strong></td>
                  <td>{row.budget}</td>
                  <td style="font-size: var(--text-sm);">{row.priority}</td>
                  <td style="font-size: var(--text-sm); color: var(--text-mid);">{row.avoid}</td>
                </tr>
              ))}
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>

        <h2 id="m-objective">Method 2 — Objective-Based Budgeting (More Accurate)</h2>
        <p>Instead of starting with a percentage, start with your revenue goal and work backwards.</p>

        <div class="formula-box">
          <p class="formula-title">Step-by-Step</p>
          <p class="formula-text" style="font-size: var(--text-base); line-height: 2;">
            1. Set revenue goal (e.g. $1,200,000)<br/>
            2. Divide by average deal value (e.g. $1,200) = 1,000 customers needed<br/>
            3. Divide by close rate (e.g. 10%) = 10,000 leads needed<br/>
            4. Multiply leads by target cost per lead (e.g. $12, illustrative) = $120,000 budget, or 10% of the revenue goal
          </p>
          <div class="formula-example">This method tells you what you <em>need</em> to spend to hit a specific goal — not just what's typical.</div>
        </div>

        <p>Use the <a href="/inbound-marketing-calculator/" style="color: var(--teal-dark);">Inbound Marketing Calculator</a> to do this calculation automatically. Enter your revenue goal and conversion rates — it outputs exactly how much traffic, leads, and marketing investment you need.</p>

        <h2 id="m-cac">Method 3 — Customer Acquisition Cost (CAC) Budgeting</h2>
        <p>The CAC method sets the budget as target new customers multiplied by the cost to acquire each one. A business that wants 120 new customers at a $250 CAC needs $30,000 in sales and marketing spend. Find your own CAC with the <a href="/cac-calculator/">CAC Calculator</a>, which divides spend by new customers and estimates payback from gross margin.</p>
        <div class="bench-table-wrap" style="margin: var(--space-4) 0;">
          <table class="bench-table">
            <thead><tr><th scope="col">Method</th><th scope="col">Inputs</th><th scope="col">Calculation</th><th scope="col">Budget</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td><strong>Percentage of revenue</strong></td><td>$800,000 revenue, 8%</td><td>$800,000 × 0.08</td><td><strong>$64,000</strong></td></tr>
              <tr style="background: var(--off-white)"><td><strong>Objective-based</strong></td><td>10,000 leads, $12 per lead</td><td>10,000 × $12</td><td><strong>$120,000</strong></td></tr>
              <tr><td><strong>CAC</strong></td><td>120 customers, $250 CAC</td><td>120 × $250</td><td><strong>$30,000</strong></td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
        <p>The three methods answer different questions, so they rarely match. The percentage method shows what businesses of this size typically spend, the objective method shows what a specific revenue goal costs, and the CAC method shows what a customer target costs. Compare all three before fixing the final number.</p>

        <h2 id="m-channels">How to Allocate Your Budget Across Channels</h2>
        <p>Once you have a total budget, the most important decision is how to split it. Here's a planning framework for a small B2B or B2C business. To split the digital share of a budget by business model and goal, use the <a href="/digital-marketing-budget-calculator/">Digital Marketing Budget Calculator</a>.</p>

        <div class="bench-table-wrap" style="margin: var(--space-4) 0;">
          <table class="bench-table">
            <thead><tr><th scope="col">Channel</th><th scope="col">% of Budget</th><th scope="col">Why</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
              {channelAlloc.map((row, i) => (
                <tr style={i % 2 !== 0 ? 'background: var(--off-white)' : ''}>
                  <td><strong>{row.channel}</strong></td>
                  <td>{row.pct}</td>
                  <td style="font-size: var(--text-sm); color: var(--text-mid);">{row.why}</td>
                </tr>
              ))}
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
        <p class="bench-source">This is a starting framework, not a rule. Your best channel mix depends on where your customers are and what you've already tested. After 90 days, shift budget toward whatever is producing the lowest CPA.</p>

        <h2 id="m-mistakes">The 4 Mistakes That Waste the Most Small Business Marketing Budget</h2>
        <p><strong>Mistake 1: Spending without tracking.</strong> If you can't tell which channel generated a sale, you'll keep funding the wrong ones. Set up UTM parameters on every link, use GA4, and connect your CRM to your advertising platforms before spending a dollar on ads.</p>
        <p><strong>Mistake 2: Spreading too thin.</strong> A $3,000/month marketing budget split across Google Ads, Facebook, Instagram, LinkedIn, TikTok, and SEO is $500 per channel — not enough to learn anything meaningful on any of them. Pick two channels, run them properly for 90 days, and only expand once you've found what works.</p>
        <p><strong>Mistake 3: Treating SEO as free.</strong> Organic search is the channel with the highest long-term ROI — but it requires consistent investment in content, technical maintenance, and link building. Expecting SEO to "just happen" without budget is how companies end up with no organic traffic after 18 months.</p>
        <p><strong>Mistake 4: Cutting in slow periods.</strong> Marketing has a compounding effect — audiences built in Q1 convert in Q3. Cutting budget during a slow quarter reduces the pipeline for every quarter that follows. If anything, slow periods are when you should maintain or increase spend, because your competitors are pulling back and acquisition costs are lower.</p>

        <h2 id="m-plan">A Simple 90-Day Plan</h2>
        <p><strong>Month 1 — Foundation.</strong> Set up tracking (GA4 + UTMs). Build or audit your website's conversion rate. Start email list capture if you haven't. This month's marketing spend goes to setup, not ads.</p>
        <p><strong>Month 2 — Test.</strong> Launch one paid channel (Google Ads for most businesses). Run a controlled budget — enough to get 50+ clicks per day — for 30 days. Don't change campaigns mid-month.</p>
        <p><strong>Month 3 — Optimise and expand.</strong> Analyse month 2 data. Pause ad groups with CPA above your target. Increase budget on what's working. Add a second channel only if month 2 was profitable.</p>

